;; murakumo.cloud participation, as decided by an aiueos node (ADR-0019).
;;
;; `kotoba/murakumo-join-plan.kotoba` mirrors `murakumo.infer.join` so a node
;; can decide its own fleet participation with no Node or JVM host present.
;; Upstream expresses those decisions over typed records, `[:option :i64]` and
;; `:string` work-kind names; the aiueos object ABI is scalar i64 only. This
;; file is the authority for the encoding that bridges the two, so neither side
;; can renumber unilaterally.
;;
;; Parity is checked, not asserted: `test/murakumo/aiueos_join_plan_parity_test.clj`
;; in kotoba-lang/murakumo drives this object across the full tier x kind x
;; memory x residency matrix and compares every result against the upstream
;; namespace. The object's own `main` additionally re-checks five vectors, so
;; it fails on the node too, not only in someone's CI.
{:format :aiueos.murakumo-node-contract/v1
 :mirrors {:repo "kotoba-lang/murakumo"
           :namespace 'murakumo.infer.join
           :source "kotoba/infer_join_core.kotoba"}

 ;; Codes are the shared wire between a `:string` kind upstream and an i64
 ;; argument here. NOT free to renumber: the parity test checks every code
 ;; against its upstream string name.
 :work-kinds {1 :media-postproc
              2 :small-shard
              3 :embarrassingly-parallel
              4 :prompt-eval
              5 :host-large-model
              6 :low-latency-pipeline
              7 :media-generate
              8 :full-shard}

 ;; Which kinds a tier answers for. Upstream reads as a surprise and is
 ;; reproduced exactly rather than "fixed": a native-tier node answers from the
 ;; NATIVE set only, so it does not accept swarm kinds such as :media-postproc
 ;; (`(if (< tier 2) (swarm-can? kind) (native-can? kind))`).
 :tiers {0 {:name :browser :swarm? true :max-resident-bytes 2147483648}
         1 {:name :wasm :swarm? true :max-resident-bytes 4294967296}
         2 {:name :native :swarm? false :max-resident-bytes 13958643712}}
 :tier-kinds {:swarm #{1 2 3 4}
              :native #{5 6 7 8}}
 :unknown-tier :native  ; matches upstream `tier-code`'s default

 ;; The kernel object ABI admits at most five parameters, so the two pairs that
 ;; always travel together are packed rather than split across calls.
 :call {:entry 'aiueos-murakumo-join-plan
        :arity 4
        :arguments [{:name :node
                     :bits {:0..1 :tier, 2 :inbound-reachable}}
                    {:name :kind :domain :work-kinds}
                    {:name :mem-request
                     ;; `present` is packed INTO the request to keep upstream's
                     ;; explicit "no -1 sentinel" property: absent memory is a
                     ;; cleared flag, never a magic byte count, so a genuine
                     ;; declaration of 0 bytes stays distinguishable from no
                     ;; declaration at all.
                     :bits {0 :declared-memory-present, :1.. :bytes}}
                    {:name :resident :unit :bytes}]
        :result {:bits {0 :eligible, 1 :needs-relay, :2.. :clamped-resident-bytes}}}

 ;; Named so they are not mistaken for solved.
 :gaps
 {:kernel-object-export
  {:state :blocked
   :why "Kernel objects export through an explicit allow-list in
         kotoba-lang/kotoba-native (`elf64.clj` `kernel-object-entries`).
         Adding entries there is a deliberate, reviewed ABI change in a third
         repo, and murakumo participation is application policy rather than
         kernel mechanism, so it likely does not belong in that ABI at all."}
  :app-catalog-admission
  {:state :blocked
   :blocked-by [:runtime-v2-context :offline-signer]
   :why "The object compiles cleanly to `x86_64-aiueos-user-v1`, but a compiled
         ELF is not yet an admissible application, for two independent reasons.

         First, `scripts/verify-kotoba-user-elf.py` pins the runtime-v2 context
         to the shape the existing smoke app has: fuel 256, the capability
         allow-bitmap byte 60 (= cap-call 2,3,4,5 from
         `kotoba/user-runtime-policy.edn`), and the cap-call trampoline at
         0x1E1020. A pure decision object declares no capabilities, so it
         produces an empty bitmap and no trampoline and is rejected -- measured,
         not assumed: `error: invalid Kotoba aiueos runtime-v2 context`.
         Admission would mean giving this object those capabilities and having
         it report its plan through them, which is a design choice about what a
         node may do with the decision, not a packaging detail.

         Second, entering the signed catalog needs the RSA signer, which is
         correctly absent from this repository -- release signatures are
         produced offline.

         Until both are settled this object is a verified artifact, not an
         admitted application."}
  :transport
  {:state :partial
   :network-stack {:state :present-qemu-proven
                   :layers [:virtio-net :dhcpv4 :dns :tcp :tls13 :https-get]
                   :physical-k16 :unverified}
   :physical-link
   {:rtl8125-pxe-handoff {:state :implemented-model-proven
                          :rings {:tx 1 :rx 1}
                          :firmware-phy-calibration :retained
                          :kernel-wiring :physical-qualification-profile
                          :physical-state :unverified}
    :why "The K16 has RTL8125, not virtio-net. The bounded handoff driver now
          takes over a firmware-initialized PXE link and pci.c selects it only
          in the diskless physical-network qualification profile. The model and
          QEMU no-device refusal are proved; this C driver has still not moved
          a frame on the board, which is what :physical-state records.

          But the last clause here used to read no K16 frame has yet been
          transmitted or received by AIUEOS, and that was false and stale.
          A DIFFERENT provider has done it. physical-kotoba-native-k16-v1.edn
          records :physical-rtl8125-provider :passed, :arp-request-reply
          :passed and :udp-positive-receipt :passed -- a DMA transmit of an
          ARP request, a DMA receive of the reply, and an Ethernet/IPv4/UDP
          receipt, on the machine. pure-kotoba-tcp-k16-v1.edn goes further and
          is :physically-verified-reproducible over ARP admission, TCP SYN,
          SYN-ACK header admission and RST.

          The distinction is per-driver, and stating it per-driver is the
          point: the Kotoba-native provider moves frames on the K16 today, the
          C PXE-handoff driver has not been run there. Read as a statement
          about AIUEOS the old sentence put the whole node further from
          working than it is."}
   :murakumo-heartbeat
   {:state :relay-enrollment-implemented-native-unverified
    :why "A hosted Murakumo resident can authenticate and heartbeat, but the
          native AIUEOS HTTP path is still a fixed GET. A request-bound UDP
          round trip to the directly attached Mac relay can now trigger
          operator-authenticated enrollment and a live-but-not-ready heartbeat.
          The operator token never enters the EFI image. Physical proof,
          device-owned persistent identity, heartbeat renewal, job execution
          and result return remain explicit gates."}
   :why "Deciding to join is not joining. QEMU proves the upper network layers;
         physical RTL8125 wiring, authenticated heartbeat and inference remain
         explicit gates."}}}
